 a private jet that once  belonged to Elvis row after row a  sprawling testament to the planes of  yesterday so many you can see them from  space is Roswell the end of the line for  most of these planes for most of the  airplanes yes Brian Hancock's company  Kabu aerospace buys old airliners they  sell the engines and strip the plane of  in-demand parts what percentage of this  plane will end up getting reused so with  the processes and procedures that are  used today we are up in the 90  percentile retirements not so great for  the rest smashed into pieces and sold  for scrap but Dave Hall sees a certain  beauty here it's quiet you hear time  passing these planes by it's got this  really eerie kind of feeling  it's a boneyard slash graveyard it's  something romantic about it in the same  sense you know to come out here and walk  amongst these Giants for nearly 20 years  he's been taking pieces of all planes  and turning them into something new this  mba detail they're cutting up today goes  to Hall Southern California workshop  where his company moto art will turn it  into furniture that borders on art a  sofa this mile-high bed or his  bestseller these tables I felt like was  really kind of like our duty to kind of  deserve aviation history a little bit  history that for now is grounded  American expects some of the planes that  were retired this week to fly again for  a different airline in different colors  but most of these planes will be parted  out and recycled some will sit here  parked in the New Mexico desert for  years to come for CBS this morning Kris  Van Cleave Roswell New Mexico  you think about how many flights they  made how many people that carry well the  ambition the opportunity they  represented and now they're quiet yeah  be like a little Museum in that area
 

